mode systems

# rng.tw: one seed, threaded, with no hidden global state.
#
# twill's randomness is seeded and deterministic by default, so a program
# reproduces. That is not the same as a *resumable* run reproducing. The
# generator has a position as well as a seed, and a run stopped after epoch 7
# and restarted has a generator sitting at position zero while the uninterrupted
# run's generator is wherever 7 epochs of shuffling left it. The two runs then
# see different batch orders and different dropout masks from epoch 8 onward,
# and every parameter after that differs.
#
# The fix here does not need the generator's position to be readable, which is
# just as well because it is not (docs/needs.md entry 6). Instead the run
# reseeds at the start of every epoch from a value derived from the base seed
# and the epoch index. Epoch 8 draws the same numbers whether it is the eighth
# epoch of one run or the first epoch after a resume, because its seed is a
# function of 8 and nothing else.
#
# The cost is that within an epoch the stream is still positional, so a run
# resumed mid-epoch is not exact. loom checkpoints on epoch boundaries only,
# and that is why.

# A 64-bit mix. Consecutive epoch indices differ in one low bit, and feeding
# base + epoch to a generator directly would give epoch 8 and epoch 9 highly
# correlated streams. The multiply-xorshift-multiply here is splitmix64's
# finaliser, which decorrelates them.
import "std/hash" as sha



# Logical right shift, because `shr` on `I64` is arithmetic: see the operators
# section of twill's docs/language-guide.md. splitmix64's finaliser shifts a
# value that is unsigned in the reference and whose top bit is set roughly half
# the time, so an arithmetic shift smears ones down and the stream diverges from
# the reference silently. Clearing the sign bit before the shift and putting it
# back at its shifted position is the same construction as twill's
# `src/float.tw` `ushr`, which exists for the same reason.
# The mixer is SHA-256, from twill's std/hash, and not the splitmix64 finaliser
# this file used to carry.
#
# splitmix64 is three 64-bit multiplies, and an I64 here is held in an F64, so
# it carries 53 bits: every multiply overflowed into the rounding and the mixer
# returned a saturated constant. Adjacent epochs then got the same seed, which
# is the one thing a seed deriver must not do, and it did it silently. See
# twill's NEEDS-2.
#
# A cryptographic hash is far more than this needs, and that is fine: seeds are
# derived a handful of times per run, not per draw.

# 52 bits taken off the front of the digest: thirteen hex digits, four bits
# each, which is inside the 53 an I64 represents exactly. Always non-negative,
# which is what `seed()` requires.
fn seed_from(s: Str) -> I64 {
  let d: Str = sha.hash_str(s)
  let out: I64 = 0
  let i: I64 = 0
  while i < 13 {
    out = out * 16 + hex_value(d[i])
    i = i + 1
  }
  out
}

fn hex_value(b: I64) -> I64 {
  if b >= 97 { return b - 87 }
  b - 48
}

# Kept for callers that want a scrambled integer from an integer. It is no
# longer splitmix64's finaliser and no longer agrees with it.
fn mix(x: I64) -> I64 = seed_from(str(x))

# The seed epoch `epoch` runs under. Deterministic in (base, epoch) alone, so
# it survives a resume and does not depend on how the run got here.
fn epoch_seed(base: I64, epoch: I64) -> I64 =
  seed_from(str(base) + ":epoch:" + str(epoch))

# Called by the trainer at the top of each epoch, before any shuffling.
fn begin_epoch(base: I64, epoch: I64) {
  seed(epoch_seed(base, epoch))
}

# A seed for a purpose that is not an epoch: a train/test split, an
# initialisation, a data augmentation stream. `tag` separates them, so a split
# and an init derived from the same base seed do not draw the same numbers.
fn derive(base: I64, tag: Str) -> I64 = seed_from(str(base) + ":" + tag)

# Initialise a model's parameters under a derived seed rather than the ambient
# one, so that changing the number of epochs does not change the starting
# weights.
fn begin_init(base: I64) {
  seed(derive(base, "init"))
}
